  "answer": "## Why your `<defs>` isn’t showing up\n\nIn this version of Material-UI, the SVG icon components ultimately render **their SVG as a fixed `<span>`/SVG structure**, and the `children` you pass is used as the “native node content” inserted into that structure—**not merged into the icon’s internal `<svg>` the way you might expect**.\n\nConcretely, `SvgIcon` (the base component behind most `material-ui/svg-icons/...` icons) takes `children`, but it places them in the returned element tree in a way that assumes `children` are **SVG drawing nodes** (like `<path/>`), not a document-level `<defs/>` block. In the provided excerpt, `SvgIcon` is implemented as:\n\n- It destructures `children` from props (`packages/material-ui/src/SvgIcon/SvgIcon.js`)\n- It then returns an element with `children` as “native” SVG content (“node passed into the el” per the excerpt). (`packages/material-ui/src/SvgIcon/SvgIcon.js`)\n\nSince your gradient `<defs>` has to be **in the `<svg>` tree before any element references it**, inserting `<defs>` at the wrong nesting level—or inside a fragment where it won’t be treated as part of the actual `<svg>` element—causes it to effectively “disappear” when the browser resolves the gradient.\n\n## How to fix (practical options)\n\n1. **Inject the gradient definition into the actual `<svg>`/Icon node that owns the `url(#MyGradient)` reference**, not just as arbitrary `children`.  \n2. If the icon component you’re using is not `SvgIcon`-based (or its children are not forwarded into the `<svg>` element), you’ll need a different approach (e.g., custom icon component or overriding the generated SVG).\n\n## Where the behavior is defined\n\n- `packages/material-ui/src/SvgIcon/SvgIcon.js` — forwards `children` into the SVG icon’s rendered output (“node passed into the el”), but it’s not designed as a “drop-in for full SVG structure like `<defs>`” (see the implementation around `children` in this file).",
